### star-flow-status/SKILL.md
--- 1
One message, three results: the conventions excerpts and the collector's digest from the two shell calls, and the spec — `<this skill's directory>/references/status_spec.md` — from a file read sent in the same message. Several tool calls in one message cost one round trip between them, not one each; only a second message costs another. That is why the two commands stay separate: a shell result past roughly 30 KB is written out to a file that costs a round trip to read back. Joined, the excerpts and the digest share one size limit, which a project with history overruns, losing both; separate, the excerpts are a fixed size and always arrive, and only the digest — the one part that grows with the history — can ever be written out. Keep the spec out of both commands for the same reason: another whole file, big enough to crowd either result toward the size limit. With no `PLAN_NAME` argument drop the `(5)` print — §5 exists to resolve one. The awk selects §7 by item number (the preamble plus items 1, 4, 5, 6, 11, 12); if it prints nothing — a stale synced copy of the conventions may number differently — load the whole section with `sed -n '/^## 7\./,/^## 8\./p'` instead. The last `awk` prints §9 and §10 and nothing else: §11, the execution branches, is deliberately not loaded — the `git branch` and `git worktree` lines above carry the live listings, and the spec restates how an execution branch and its worktree read. Every path the digest prints is relative to the project root. The digest is: every plan's frontmatter, its `## Sub-plans` index and its §3/§5 placeholder counts (`[TBD]` and `【待定】` together); every run log's frontmatter, its body counted per heading, and the dates in it; the frontmatter of every listed artifact outside a run directory; and a depth-1 listing of `metds/` and `wkdrs/`. Together with the spec and the conventions sections, that is the whole input for Steps 2–9.
