### star-refs-reviewer/references/survey_spec.md
--- 3
2. **Search wide.** Step 1's machinery as written — same queries from the profile, same delegation cap and formats, same budgets and caching per `references/source_policy.md` — but the pool is kept, not cut to 15: a survey cites breadth. Keep the count ledger as the pool moves: found → deduplicated → screened → tiered, recorded in the survey's Scope section.
3. **Screen and tier.** Three reading tiers, by what each may support. **deep** — 8–12 papers at `method-and-results` or better; only these may fill comparison-table rows or carry reproduced numbers. **abstract** — 15–25 papers at `abstract-and-intro`, enough to place a paper on a branch and characterize it in a clause; fetch a page only where the search record carries no abstract. **record** — the rest, cited from their records' facts (title, venue, year) for breadth and trends, never characterized, never in a table. Tiering weighs relevance and impact score together; unlike the full pass, the score decides selection, so a branch's top-scored works belong in deep or abstract, not record. The list shows each score, partial until that page is read. Then the mode's one judgment call, in a single direct question: the profile, the taxonomy axis (and the axis rejected), and the tiered list with the deep tier recommended. `involve=low` takes the recommendation unasked and logs it in the decisions record (conventions §7.8); the full pass's core-set question stays mandatory.
4. **Read.** Read with delegation where it helps — when the deep- and abstract-tier papers can be read independently and read-only, one paper per delegate, each given its own share of the per-host request budget written as a number per `references/source_policy.md` and one `raw/` prefix per paper, each returning the survey collector format in `references/refs_rubric.md`. Below 6 papers to read, no delegation at all — read them in the main session. A paper whose fetched page names an official repository gets the same main-agent GitHub call as Step 3, completing its score — deep tier as a rule, an abstract-tier paper only when its page was fetched anyway; no page is fetched just to find a repo. No figure is captured here: figures are Step 3's, belonging to a note this mode does not write.
+++ 3
2. **Search wide.** Step 1's machinery as written — same queries from the profile, same fan-out cap and formats, same budgets and caching per `references/source_policy.md` — but the pool is kept, not cut to 15: a survey cites breadth. Keep the count ledger as the pool moves: found → deduplicated → screened → tiered, recorded in the survey's Scope section.
3. **Screen and tier.** Three reading tiers, by what each may support. **deep** — 8–12 papers at `method-and-results` or better; only these may fill comparison-table rows or carry reproduced numbers. **abstract** — 15–25 papers at `abstract-and-intro`, enough to place a paper on a branch and characterize it in a clause; fetch a page only where the search record carries no abstract. **record** — the rest, cited from their records' facts (title, venue, year) for breadth and trends, never characterized, never in a table. Tiering weighs relevance and impact score together; unlike the full pass, the score decides selection, so a branch's top-scored works belong in deep or abstract, not record. The list shows each score, partial until that page is read. Then the mode's one judgment call, in a single question: the profile, the taxonomy axis (and the axis rejected), and the tiered list with the deep tier recommended. `involve=low` takes the recommendation unasked and logs it in the decisions record (conventions §7.8); the full pass's core-set question stays mandatory.
4. **Read.** The deep and abstract tiers may fan out as Step 3 does — one paper per collector, per-host budget split as numbers per `references/source_policy.md`, one `raw/` prefix per paper — each returning the survey collector format in `references/refs_rubric.md`. Below 6 papers to read, skip the fan-out and read them in the main session. A paper whose fetched page names an official repository gets the same main-agent GitHub call as Step 3, completing its score — deep tier as a rule, an abstract-tier paper only when its page was fetched anyway; no page is fetched just to find a repo. No figure is captured here: figures are Step 3's, belonging to a note this mode does not write.
### star-refs-reviewer/references/survey_spec.md
--- 1
8. **Digest.** Under about 500 words: the source and profile, the count ledger, the branches written, the self-check result, and the routing — each read-next paper is one `star-refs-reviewer <arxiv-id>`, the whole list one `star-refs-reviewer add …`; positioning goes to `star-plan-coach` §2 (Related Work & Positioning), or §1 (Problem Definition & Motivation) where the map shows the gap the plan wanted already closed, and `star-idea-storm` where there is no plan yet to reopen; the full pass builds the notes and verified bib when the project needs them.
